                    },
                    {
                        "form_id": "10380181-409f-4db4-9f2d-e2d6370388ea",
                        "entry_id": "4bcaa8ce-2d10-300c-9106-2759ae203c40",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "Marathas"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "176c2e30-6d37-397f-a4fc-cc3fd31f39d5",
                        "entry_id": "4bcaa8ce-2d10-300c-9106-2759ae203c40",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A Hindu caste (or caste cluster) that is particularly associated with the Indian state of Maharashtra; a member of said caste."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "0eb87d2d-f861-4a32-9392-a45c9efef54b",
                                "sense_id": "176c2e30-6d37-397f-a4fc-cc3fd31f39d5",
                                "text": "2004, D. L. Sheth, Ram Manohar Lohia on Caste in Indian Politics, [extracts from 1996, Lokayan Bulletin], Ghanshyam Shah (editor), Caste and Democratic Politics in India, Wimbledon Publishing Company (Anthem Press), page 81,\nThe Marathas are a peculiar caste of Maharashtra, who claim to be Kshatriyas but are more like the cultivator-Sudras of north India."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "15c01138-d899-43c8-b588-9b540f9d59d4",
                                "sense_id": "176c2e30-6d37-397f-a4fc-cc3fd31f39d5",
                                "text": "2004, Kumar Suresh Singh (general editor), B. V. Bhanu, et al. (editors), People of India: Maharashtra, Volume 30, Part 2, Anthropological Survey of India, Popular Prakashan, page 1434,\nThe Maratha are a numerically dominant caste in Maharashtra. As said earlier, Maratha is not a caste but a caste cluster, the main component[s] of which are the Marathas proper and the Kunbis or Kulwadis. […] The Maratha speak mainly Marathi, the language of the region."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "54c2bae6-d58e-40b6-8156-7bb280b05576",
                                "sense_id": "176c2e30-6d37-397f-a4fc-cc3fd31f39d5",
                                "text": "The latter^([of two elements of anti-casteism]) was dominated by the Maratha caste and a Maratha leadership and was supported by the Maratha ruler of Kolhapur, Chattrapati Shahu, appealing primarily to Maratha-Kunbi cluster in its struggle against upper caste domination."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "81fc8f81-5d74-3c87-93dd-b7049e38255e",
                        "entry_id": "4bcaa8ce-2d10-300c-9106-2759ae203c40",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A Marathi-speaking people particularly associated with Maharashtra; a member of said people (regardless of caste); frequently used attributively."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "14a09007-a4b0-4a8c-b9ae-5070e4581e13",
                                "sense_id": "81fc8f81-5d74-3c87-93dd-b7049e38255e",
                                "text": "(3) Maratha vessels were to have free access to all French ports, and vice versa."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b54b774f-f3e0-47f5-90b9-35d0c9c3ef21",
                                "sense_id": "81fc8f81-5d74-3c87-93dd-b7049e38255e",
                                "text": "2023, Sangita Govindrao Ghar, Marathas Contribution in Deccan Plateau, Lulu, page 1,\nThe first major threat to Moghul imperial power came from a Hindu tribal confederacy known as the Marathas. Located in the mountainous regions of the Deccan, the Marathas were mainly drawn from the lowest caste of society, but they became a powerfully militant community under their ruler, King Sivaji, who died in 1680."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "5d64a4b6-19cf-4682-9ce3-a18ce563583d",
                                "sense_id": "81fc8f81-5d74-3c87-93dd-b7049e38255e",
                                "text": "Then around 2 pm, a detachment of Maratha irregular horse approached and engaged the allied contingent of Mysore horse that were riding forward outpost duty for the British."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
